{
 "schema": 1,
 "slug": "failover",
 "title": "Kill a relay, watch it heal",
 "question": "When the radio carrying my traffic dies, how long until traffic flows again?",
 "headlines": [
  {
   "value": "7.0 s",
   "label": "median time to reroute",
   "detail": "worst 9.4 s over 10 failures, default settings"
  },
  {
   "value": "0.7 s",
   "label": "worst case with keep-alives",
   "detail": "250 ms heartbeat, +46% control traffic"
  },
  {
   "value": "0.3 s",
   "label": "to rejoin after a reboot",
   "detail": "the relay comes back with an empty routing table"
  }
 ],
 "summary": "A field team streams to HQ across a twelve-radio grid. The relay carrying the stream is powered off mid-transfer, and the mesh routes around it with no operator action. In the run charted here it took 8.4 s (73 packets lost) to move from team \u2192 n13 \u2192 n02 \u2192 n01 \u2192 hq to team \u2192 n12 \u2192 n02 \u2192 n01 \u2192 hq. How long healing takes is a design choice. With routing adverts alone, a dead relay is noticed only after six adverts go missing, so the worst case grows with the advert interval. Keep-alive heartbeats catch the failure after three misses for a fraction of the airtime: at 250 ms they cap recovery at 0.7 s, where adverts alone needed 9.4 s.",
